WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.840
<v Riley>Hey there — on today's episode, two teenage sisters in the NWT get pulled into a shelter dorm despite a federal law that's supposed to hand child welfare authority back to their own Nation. So we're asking: five years after that law passed, who actually holds the pen on decisions like that? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:38.315 --> 00:01:00.162
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Thursday, September 17th, 2026. Speaking to you from the traditional territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations here in the Cariboo region. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation.

00:01:00.456 --> 00:01:40.369
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're looking at who actually holds the pen when it comes to land, water, and governance decisions in this region. Not just who gets consulted after the fact. Quick weather check first. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's mainly clear and around 9 degrees. High of 21 later, though it'll cloud over through the day. No rain expected. Around the region, 100 Mile House is sitting near 9 with clear skies, Williams Lake around 8 and overcast, Quesnel near 9 and clear, Alexis Creek cooler at 6 with clear skies.

00:01:40.664 --> 00:01:46.546
<v Riley>Bundle up for the morning chill. Let's get into what's happening around the region.

00:01:47.946 --> 00:02:14.648
<v Riley>Fifteen stories on the list today, five of them close to home, which is a fuller local slate than we've had most of this month. Starting there. The Log Loader competition in Williams Lake raised more than ten thousand dollars for Cariboo Memorial Hospital. Operators competed with heavy equipment on September 12th and 13th at the Harvest Fair, stacking and moving logs against the clock, and the money goes straight to the hospital foundation.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:19.231
<v Casey>Heavy machinery for a good cause. Hard to argue with that one.

00:02:19.483 --> 00:02:22.009
<v Riley>Over to you for the courts file.

00:02:22.262 --> 00:02:40.304
<v Casey>A trial date has now been set for a man charged in connection with a drug bust in Williams Lake. Fifty-five-year-old Jason Bruneau is due back in court on February 22nd, 2027. Two full days have been set aside, and he'll be tried by a provincial judge alone, no jury.

00:02:40.557 --> 00:03:02.207
<v Riley>A prescribed burn is planned west of Williams Lake, near the north end of Cotton Road by Riske Creek, and it could start as early as September 18th. Crews are telling residents to expect visible smoke and flame in the area, with periodic burning possible through to the end of October, and reduced visibility on nearby roads while it's underway.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:08.233
<v Casey>Separate that from wildfire smoke in your head — this one is timed, monitored, and deliberate.

00:03:08.486 --> 00:03:26.167
<v Casey>A structure fire also hit a hotel in Quesnel Tuesday night. The Quesnel Fire Department responded to the call and had two engines on scene for just over an hour. Damage was limited to the corner of one building, nobody was hurt, and the cause is still under investigation.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:46.627
<v Riley>And if you want something slower than any of that, the 100 Mile Free Press ran its regular outdoors column this week. Doug Porter writing about a day on the water. Not breaking news by any measure, but the lakes are still good for the drive before the season turns, and that's basically the whole column.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:53.014
<v Casey>A column about doing nothing productive on a lake is the most rural item on the list.

00:03:53.267 --> 00:04:13.835
<v Casey>Further afield now. On Vancouver Island, bluebirds are singing in the Cowichan Valley again after going locally extinct decades ago. Habitat loss from development wiped them out entirely. Seventy landowners and a wave of volunteers put up nest boxes and restored the open grassland habitat those birds need, and the population has come back over several years.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:19.861
<v Riley>Seventy landowners decided one songbird was worth a decade of nest boxes. Somebody made that call.

00:04:20.114 --> 00:04:41.403
<v Riley>In B.C., a man who flatlined en route to hospital is alive today because of a new provincial paramedic training program. Advanced-care paramedics are now trained to administer thrombolysis — clot-dissolving medication — for heart attacks in the field, before the patient reaches an emergency room. His case is the one the Vancouver Sun used to illustrate the program.

00:04:41.656 --> 00:04:46.708
<v Casey>That changes an actual outcome, not a workflow chart in a health authority binder.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:05:01.033
<v Casey>McClatchy, the U.S. newspaper chain that owns the Miami Herald, made deep cuts to newsrooms around the country. The company says subscriber interest did not support its current level of investment. One employee called the layoffs a blood bath.

00:05:01.286 --> 00:05:06.699
<v Riley>Fewer reporters means fewer people asking the questions the rest of us end up quoting.

00:05:06.951 --> 00:05:27.158
<v Riley>Sticking with birds for one more story. Researchers think they've solved a decades-old mystery about why some songbirds appear to migrate in the wrong direction entirely. Tiny trackers, no bigger than a breath mint, suggest the behaviour traces back to migratory routes set during the last Ice Age and never fully updated as the glaciers retreated.

00:05:27.411 --> 00:05:30.298
<v Casey>Ancient muscle memory, still running the old map.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:48.232
<v Casey>The BC College of Family Physicians is raising alarm about fragmented medical records. The province has more than forty different electronic medical record systems in use, and they don't connect. Doctors call that a patient safety risk, not an inconvenience, especially when someone moves between clinics or between towns.

00:05:48.484 --> 00:05:54.619
<v Riley>If you've ever recited your own medical history from memory in a new clinic, that's the reason.

00:05:54.871 --> 00:06:09.666
<v Riley>California is reconsidering its long-standing opposition to nuclear power. The state is weighing whether to extend the life of the Diablo Canyon plant and allow construction of new reactors, driven by concern it won't meet its clean electricity goals without them.

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:16.775
<v Casey>A state famous for shutting reactors down, now negotiating with its own past decisions. Worth watching where that lands.

00:06:17.027 --> 00:06:31.100
<v Casey>Samsung has quietly ended software support for the Galaxy Watch 4 and Watch 4 Classic, the two devices that arguably saved Wear OS as a platform. They'll keep functioning, but no new features and no security patches going forward.

00:06:31.353 --> 00:06:37.848
<v Riley>Hardware that still works fine but stops getting patches — that's the hidden cost of a smart device.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:07:00.112
<v Riley>And if you've ever tried to send a decent photo from an Android phone to an iPhone and had it arrive compressed and blurry, Engadget has a fix. Set your media sharing to "HD plus original quality" instead of the default "HD optimized for chat," and the file comes through at full resolution, at the cost of a bigger file size.

00:07:00.365 --> 00:07:05.056
<v Casey>Filed under things nobody tells you until the wedding photos already look terrible.

00:07:05.308 --> 00:07:16.494
<v Casey>Astronomers have found eighty-four unusually bright objects hiding in nearby galaxies, visible only in a wavelength of light most telescopes can barely detect. Nobody's confirmed yet what these objects actually are.

00:07:16.747 --> 00:07:44.893
<v Riley>Closing on one from the New York Times. A piece on security guards — the people posted outside synagogues, mosques, and other vulnerable buildings after a string of threats and a high-profile shooting outside a mosque. The paper found entry-level pay averaging around fifteen dollars and thirty-five cents an hour including tips and overtime, based on a small sample of salaries, with one-to-four-year veterans averaging about eighteen fifty-six. Unions are pushing for better pay and real training standards.

00:07:45.145 --> 00:07:50.197
<v Casey>We're asking more of those workers every year and paying them like nothing changed.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:58.027
<v Riley>That question — who gets protected, and who decides — is where the Deep Dive is headed. Before that, the spotlight.

00:07:59.428 --> 00:08:28.155
<v Riley>Today we're highlighting the Cariboo Chilcotin Métis Association, Williams Lake's chartered Métis community. They run cultural programs, advocacy on behalf of Métis members across the region, and gatherings through the year, including their long-running Métis Music Jamboree, which has become a fixture on the Cariboo calendar. If you're Métis and looking for community, or just curious about the culture and history here, reach out to them directly — that's the fastest way in.

00:08:28.431 --> 00:08:34.728
<v Casey>Decades of steady programming without ever needing a national headline is its own kind of achievement.

00:08:36.128 --> 00:08:52.841
<v Casey>Everything we're about to get into comes down to one thing. When somebody decides what gets saved — a language, a watershed, a family staying together — who is in the room for that decision, and who gets the outcome read to them afterward?

00:08:53.107 --> 00:09:12.480
<v Riley>Start with the story out of the Northwest Territories. A Tłı̨chǫ mother told APTN her two teenage daughters were removed from care and placed not with kin, not in a foster home, but in a youth emergency shelter in Yellowknife. Dormitory-style rooms. Both girls said directly that they weren't comfortable there.

00:09:12.746 --> 00:09:20.723
<v Casey>The shelter is called Home Base. Which is quite a name for a place the kids describe as not a home.

00:09:20.988 --> 00:09:42.260
<v Riley>Bill C-92 was written for exactly this. Federal law since January 1st, 2020 — it affirms the inherent right of Indigenous communities to run their own child and family services, and it was designed specifically to address how badly overrepresented Indigenous kids are in state care. That's not a pilot project, that's jurisdiction recognized in statute.

00:09:42.526 --> 00:10:09.496
<v Casey>Five years in, and in the Northwest Territories, ninety-eight percent of children in government care are Indigenous. That's from the territorial government's own reporting, not my arithmetic. And the GNWT acknowledges the overrepresentation in its own documents. So the law says jurisdiction moves to communities, and the lived reality is two teenagers in a shelter dorm because a community-run kinship or foster alternative either doesn't exist yet or can't take them.

00:10:09.762 --> 00:10:35.212
<v Riley>Before C-92, a Nation had no legal footing at all to stand up its own child welfare authority with real decision-making power. It could advocate, it could pressure, it couldn't govern. A coordination agreement under the act changes what a Nation can demand at the funding table, and legal analysts tracking implementation describe it as uneven rather than empty — some Nations are further along than others.

00:10:35.478 --> 00:10:55.231
<v Casey>Uneven is the polite word. Ground to stand on isn't a building with staff in it. You need trained caseworkers, a roster of kinship homes run by community members, and an operating budget that doesn't depend on a ministry re-approving it every fiscal year. None of that showed up for this family.

00:10:55.497 --> 00:11:21.707
<v Riley>Compare it to the bluebirds for a second. Seventy landowners in the Cowichan Valley decided a songbird was worth ten years of nest boxes, and nobody had to grant them authority to act. Child welfare is the opposite — you cannot volunteer your way into the legal right to decide where a child sleeps. The law has to move first. That's why C-92 matters even when the capacity lags.

00:11:21.973 --> 00:11:40.586
<v Casey>Except the law moving first is also how you get five years of paperwork and a fifteen-year-old in a dormitory this month. Volunteers with nest boxes had something the Tłı̨chǫ Government doesn't: nobody controlling their budget. Recognition without money is permission to solve a problem at your own expense.

00:11:40.851 --> 00:12:07.441
<v Riley>So take the corridor, where there's no child in the middle of it. The B.C. Rail Corridor Alliance is pressing the province for funding and a new governance structure now that CN is ending its lease on the old rail corridor. Mayors from across the region — 100 Mile House Mayor Maureen Pinkney among them — spoke at UBCM on Tuesday asking for money and a seat at the table.

00:12:07.707 --> 00:12:31.638
<v Casey>And that corridor runs for hundreds of kilometres through Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in and other territories. I went looking for First Nations named as governance partners in that Alliance and didn't find them in what's been published. Maybe they're at the table and the coverage left it out. Maybe they aren't there. Either way, the reporting frames this as a municipal-versus-province fight over infrastructure control.

00:12:31.904 --> 00:12:54.316
<v Riley>Which is precisely the moment to build co-governance in. This is a governance model being drafted from scratch, not inherited from a century-old rail charter. Nobody has divided up the seats yet. Retrofitting a nation into a structure ten years after the bylaws are set is the hard version; writing it into the founding documents is the easy one.

00:12:54.581 --> 00:13:20.412
<v Casey>Every new body says that at the start. Then the founding documents get written by whoever showed up to the first three meetings. And notice who's making the ask here — rural municipalities who felt blindsided when CN decided the corridor's future without them. They know exactly what being left out of a decision feels like, which makes it a fair question whether they're extending the courtesy downstream.

00:13:20.678 --> 00:13:44.608
<v Riley>That's an answerable question rather than a rhetorical one, though, and that's the useful part. The Alliance is barely formed. Somebody covering the next Alliance meeting, or the next UBCM session, can ask on the record whether the nations along that corridor hold a standing seat, a formal consultation slot, or nothing yet. An open structure is a structure you can still influence.

00:13:44.874 --> 00:13:57.030
<v Casey>Agreed on the asking. I'd just note that a consultation slot and a seat are different animals, and the difference tends to show up at the first vote that costs somebody money.

00:13:57.296 --> 00:14:25.025
<v Riley>Then the water story, which is the one that arrives looking like pure upside. A research team has built a solar-powered desalination system that pulls fresh water out of seawater while removing almost all the leftover salt as a solid, instead of pumping brine back into the ocean. It's self-cleaning, and it can recover valuable minerals like lithium in the process. For a coastal community without reliable freshwater, that's a real technical leap.

00:14:25.291 --> 00:14:51.121
<v Casey>Canada has long-term drinking water advisories on reserves that have sat unresolved for years. The bottleneck was never that nobody had invented the right membrane. H2O Innovation has built custom treatment systems for First Nations water needs, University of Regina researchers have worked on portable units — the technology has largely existed for a while. The scarce resource is a funded technician who's still there in year six.

00:14:51.387 --> 00:15:08.481
<v Riley>The mineral recovery is what makes this one structurally different, though. A system that produces a saleable byproduct has a revenue line attached to it. That's the piece every failed pilot was missing — a reason for the thing to keep running after the announcement.

00:15:08.747 --> 00:15:34.957
<v Casey>A water plant with a side business in lithium is a beautiful sentence. So is "the new corridor governance will be inclusive." Lithium prices swing hard, and whoever wrote the funding agreement decides who owns the mineral stream — the community, the equipment vendor, or the program that bought the unit. If that clause reads wrong, you've built a revenue line that flows past the people drinking the water.

00:15:35.223 --> 00:15:42.440
<v Riley>Which is a contract question, not a physics question. And contract questions can be won at the drafting stage.

00:15:42.706 --> 00:15:51.442
<v Casey>They can also be lost there, quietly, by people who weren't in the room. Same failure mode as the shelter and the corridor.

00:15:51.708 --> 00:16:12.221
<v Riley>That's the thread that actually ties these three together, and it isn't the one I'd have predicted. The pattern isn't "no technology exists" or "no law recognizes the right." Both exist. The operating capacity sits somewhere else — inside a ministry or a funder that isn't accountable to the community living with the result.

00:16:12.486 --> 00:16:27.681
<v Casey>The desalination unit answers to whoever funded the pilot. The Yellowknife shelter answers to the GNWT. The corridor, as currently proposed, answers to a province-and-municipality alliance. None of those three answer to the Nation whose members live with the outcome.

00:16:27.947 --> 00:16:43.900
<v Riley>So the design question for any of them isn't whether the technology works, or even whether a law recognizes jurisdiction. It's who holds operational control, and who that operator picks up the phone for when something breaks at two in the morning.

00:16:44.166 --> 00:17:12.276
<v Casey>And whether you can check the answer yourself. Does the Tłı̨chǫ Government run its own child and family services agency with its own hired staff, yes or no. Does any Cariboo nation hold a formal seat on the corridor alliance, yes or no. We can't verify either from where we sit — we don't have the Alliance's membership list, and we don't know whether a coordination agreement is in force or still being negotiated.

00:17:12.542 --> 00:17:41.791
<v Riley>Both of which are a phone call away, which is more than most policy questions offer. A band office, a local reporter, a listener with ten spare minutes. If you have either answer — or you're working inside one of those files — send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a and we'll put it on air, because that's a far better test of whether jurisdiction moved than how a bill sounded the day it passed.

00:17:42.056 --> 00:17:58.390
<v Casey>What shifted for me is the drafting-stage argument — new structures are easier to open than old ones. I'll still believe jurisdiction moved when somebody can name the caseworker and the technician. Until then it's authority on paper. Good talking it through, Riley.

00:17:58.656 --> 00:18:14.230
<v Riley>And that's a question we can keep asking out loud. That's Thursday — crews may be out west of Williams Lake with that prescribed burn as early as tomorrow, so keep your windows shut and your questions loud. Take care, everyone.

00:18:14.496 --> 00:18:31.970
<v Casey>Look, I'll be straight with you—we didn't get First Nations communications folks in the room for this one, and that's something we should fix. If there's someone from a Nation in the Cariboo listening who wants to come talk about this stuff, we're here for it.